  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="79"/>
    (1B  parameters)  ( Figure  6B-C;  Supplemental Table  8 ),  demonstrating  the  efficiency  of  our foundation model framework. The largest model, PlantCAD2-L, achieved the best AUROC for binary leaf expression and the highest Spearman correlation for absolute expression prediction.
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="97"/>
    <location value="455"/>
    <location value="275"/>
    Given that regulatory information also lies outside the proximal promoter 38 ,  we evaluated the effect of varying input window sizes on gene expression prediction. Increasing the window from 1 kb to 4 kb both upstream of the transcription start site and downstream of the transcription stop site resulted in measurable improvements, raising the AUROC from 0.8221 to 0.8455 for binary leaf expression task and  Spearman correlation from 0.6296 to 0.6455 for absolute expression prediction task on the NAM test set ( Figure S6 ). These improvements highlight the role of distal enhancers  and  long-range  motifs  in  shaping  expression.  However,  previous  studies  in  both humans and plants have shown that current deep learning models lack the resolution to capture allele-specific  effects 62 -64 .  We  therefore  tested  whether  fine-tuning  a  foundation model  could mitigate this limitation by evaluating per-orthogroup correlations within the NAM population, a comparison  sensitive  to  allelic  differences.  Consistent  with  prior  findings 62 -64 ,  only  marginal improvements were observed for leaf absolute expression prediction, with the median Spearman correlation increasing from 0.112 (supervised CNN+LSTM) to 0.140 (PlantCAD2-S) ( Figure S7 ). These  results  suggest  that  achieving  allele-specific  resolution  will  likely  require  specialized training strategies, such as explicitly modeling cis-regulatory variants 65,66 .
  </text>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="3"/>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="294"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="459"/>
    Translation prediction was based on ribosome profiling (ribo-seq), a sequencing-based approach that  estimates  translation  activity  by  mapping  ribosome-protected mRNA  fragments.  Because ribo-seq data are scarce in plants, we restricted training to Arabidopsis and tested both withinspecies  performance  and  cross-species  transfer  to  maize.  Interestingly,  although  supervised PlantCAD2-S (88M) is much larger than the CNN-LSTM (~1.7M), the latter performed better, suggesting that large supervised models are prone to overfitting when trained on limited data. By contrast,  fine-tuned  PlantCAD2  with  parameter-efficient LoRA  maintained robust performance without signs of overfitting. However, cross-species regression of absolute translation levels was less effective ( Figure 6F ), suggesting that the model may have captured noise inherent in riboseq -based  quantitative  estimates.  Encouraged  by  the  strong  transfer  observed  for  binary classification  of  leaf  translation,  we  next  tested  whether  gene  expression  could  similarly  be transferred from Arabidopsis to maize. Using a separate Arabidopsis gene expression dataset  67 with 1,024 bp upstream and downstream sequences, we found that direct transfer performed poorly  (AUROC:  0.786  in  Arabidopsis  vs.  0.631  in  maize)  compared  to  protein  abundance
  </text>
  <page_break/>
  <text>
    <thread thread_id="3"/>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="116"/>
    prediction (0.790 in Arabidopsis vs. 0.692 in maize) ( Figure S8 ). This contrast may highlight a 487 fundamental  difference  between  regulatory  layers:  translational  control  appears  to  be  more 488 evolutionarily  conserved  than  transcriptional  regulation.  Consistent  with  evidence  that  protein 489 abundance is under stronger selective constraint than transcript levels 63 , these results explain 490 why translation prediction transfers effectively across species, whereas accurate gene expression 491 prediction requires phylogenetically diverse training data. 492
  </text>
