<![CDATA[conserved, while those with identity scores <15 were labeled as neutral. Sites with intermediate identity scores or insufficient coverage were excluded from evaluation to ensure high-confidence labels 23 .]]>  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="97"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="226"/>
<![CDATA[The second task utilized conservation scores derived from multiple sequence alignments (MSAs) of  orthologous  coding  sequences  from  325  Poaceae  genomes,  a  high-quality  subset  of  the recently  published  set  of  727  genomes.  Using Pharus latifolius as  an  outgroup  species,  gap columns were removed prior to conservation estimates. Using PHAST  77 , PhyloP scores were calculated per site based on a neutral model derived from fourfold degenerate sites and 'LRT' methods  with  the  mode  'CONACC'.  Sites  with  phyloP  scores  above  5  were  classified  as conserved, while those below 1.5 were considered neutral. Sites with intermediate scores were excluded to maintain label clarity. For TIS sites, we retained all 36,668 (26,653 conserved vs 10,015  less conserved)  sites given their biological importance. For non-TIS  sites,  we downsampled to 183,687  sites  (103,369  conserved  versus  80,318  neutral)  for  computational efficiency while maintaining the conserved-to-neutral ratio.]]>  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="244"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="300"/>
<![CDATA[For both tasks, the evaluated site was centered (4096th) within a 8,192 bp input sequence, and the reference base at that position was masked. The model's predicted likelihood of the reference allele  was  extracted  and  used  as  the  zero-shot  conservation  score.  Higher  likelihoods  were hypothesized to reflect stronger conservation. Model performance was assessed using AUROC, comparing scores between conserved and neutral sites.]]>  </text>
  <heading level="4">
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="317"/>
    <location value="408"/>
    <location value="325"/>
    Core and non-core gene classification using the zero-shot strategy
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="334"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="446"/>
    To assess the ability of PlantCAD2 to distinguish between core and non-core genes in a population, a zero-shot strategy was applied to classify within species conservation of genes in maize and tomato. For maize, the pangene table derived from 26 Nested Association Mapping genomes   78  was used. Core genes were defined as those present in all 26 NAM genomes, whereas non-core genes included both dispensable genes (present in 2-23 genomes) and private genes (present in only one genome). For genes with multiple transcripts, the canonical transcript specified in the annotation was used. For tomato,  the  pan-genome  dataset  assembled  from  586  high-quality  genomes  79   was  used.  Genes present in all 586 accessions were defined as core genes, and non-core genes consist of dispensable (present in 6-580 accessions) and private (present in less than 5 accessions). The longest transcript was selected to represent each gene across all analyses.
  </text>
  <page_break/>
  <page_header>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="45"/>
    <location value="4"/>
    <location value="466"/>
    <location value="19"/>
    available under aCC-BY-NC 4.0 International license. (which was not certified by peer review) is the author/funder, who has granted bioRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. It is made bioRxiv preprint doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.08.27.672609; this version posted September 1, 2025. The copyright holder for this preprint
  </page_header>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="113"/>
<![CDATA[To quantify the model's prediction at each functional junction, a masked motif accuracy score was 631 defined.  For  example,  to  evaluate  translation  initiation  sites,  the  canonical  ATG  start  codon  was 632 masked, and the model's predicted likelihoods for the three masked nucleotides were extracted and 633 averaged. A similar approach was applied to other junction types, including translation termination 634 sites  (TAA,  TAG,  TGA),  splice  donor  sites  (GT),  and  splice  acceptor  sites  (AG),  by  masking  the 635 corresponding motifs and calculating average token likelihoods. 636]]>  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="118"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="124"/>
    637
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="130"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="136"/>
    638
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="141"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="148"/>
    639
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="166"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="172"/>
    640
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="130"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="148"/>
    To evaluate performance, core genes were treated as positive and non-core genes as negative, and AUROC was calculated based on the masked motif accuracy scores for each gene.
  </text>
  <heading level="4">
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="164"/>
    <location value="264"/>
    <location value="172"/>
    Accessible chromatin region prediction
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="182"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="225"/>
    To evaluate the capability  of  PlantCAD2  to  capture  regulatory  sequence  features,  fine-tuning 641 experiments were performed using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)  60  on two accessible chromatin 642 prediction tasks: (1) cross-species accessible regions (ACRs) prediction, and (2) cell-type-specific 643 ACR prediction. 644
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="231"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="238"/>
    645
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="243"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="323"/>
    For the cross-species task, ATAC-seq peak regions from 12 plant species  59  were downloaded 646 from NCBI (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE128434). We followed the 647 data processing pipeline as described by Wrightsman et al  80 . For each species, peak regions 648 were processed by extracting the midpoint of each peak and symmetrically extending it by half 649 the target input window size (300bp, 600bp and 1,000bp) in both directions to generate positive 650 observations. To reflect the real-world scenario in which most of the genome is inaccessible, the 651 rest of the genome was used as negative examples, ensuring no overlap with known peaks. 652
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="329"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="336"/>
    653
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="342"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="348"/>
    654
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="354"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="360"/>
    655
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="366"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="373"/>
    656
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="378"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="385"/>
    657
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="391"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="397"/>
    658
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="60"/>
    <location value="341"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="397"/>
    For  the  cell-type-specific  task,  we  used  the  single-cell  ATAC-Seq 61 and  used  a  similar preprocessing pipeline, but each genomic region could be associated with accessibility across 92 cell types. As such, the task was framed as a multi-label classification problem, where each region was assigned a binary accessibility label for each of the 92 cell types based on its overlap with experimentally identified peaks.
  </text>
  <heading level="6">
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="414"/>
    <location value="244"/>
    <location value="422"/>
    Gene expression prediction in leaf 659
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="30"/>
    <location value="431"/>
    <location value="454"/>
    <location value="462"/>
<![CDATA[To  evaluate  the  models'  ability  to  predict  gene  expression,  we  designed  two  tasks:  (1)  leaf 660 absolute expression and (2) leaf on/off expression classification. The training dataset was derived 661 from 15 Andropogoneae species  62 . For validation, we held out two species closest to Zea mays -662]]>  </text>
